1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,348
Every time you speak English, you are speaking the language of war.

2
00:00:04,348 --> 00:00:08,426
Invaders, raiders, conquerors and soldiers all left their words behind.

3
00:00:08,426 --> 00:00:15,261
And one October morning, a single battle pushed English out of the palaces of its own country, for three hundred years.

4
00:00:16,561 --> 00:00:19,760
Today, about one point five billion people speak English.

5
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:25,989
But it started on one small island, and it was shaped, again and again, by the people who came to fight for it.

6
00:00:27,289 --> 00:00:29,560
The evidence is hiding in plain sight.

7
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:30,337
The word sky.

8
00:00:30,337 --> 00:00:31,174
The word beef.

9
00:00:31,174 --> 00:00:32,011
The word loot.

10
00:00:32,011 --> 00:00:32,907
The word blitz.

11
00:00:32,907 --> 00:00:35,298
Each one is a scar from a different war.

12
00:00:35,298 --> 00:00:38,167
In this film, we follow those scars, one by one.

13
00:00:39,467 --> 00:00:40,142
Chapter one.

14
00:00:40,142 --> 00:00:40,985
Before English.

15
00:00:40,985 --> 00:00:44,693
Two thousand years ago, nobody in Britain spoke a word of English.

16
00:00:44,693 --> 00:00:49,301
The people here were Celtic tribes, and their language was closer to modern Welsh.

17
00:00:50,601 --> 00:00:52,386
Julius Caesar had come and gone.

18
00:00:52,386 --> 00:00:58,578
But in the year forty three, Rome returned under the emperor Claudius, and this time, the legions came to stay.

19
00:00:59,878 --> 00:01:02,292
From Rome, through Gaul, and across the sea.

20
00:01:02,292 --> 00:01:06,298
The Romans built a new town on the river Thames, and called it Londinium.

21
00:01:06,298 --> 00:01:07,834
You know it today as London.

22
00:01:09,134 --> 00:01:11,526
In the north, the tribes never surrendered.

23
00:01:11,526 --> 00:01:18,199
So in one hundred and twenty two, the emperor Hadrian ordered a wall, right across the island, to mark where Rome ended.

24
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:24,135
Rome gave the future English language some of its oldest borrowed words.

25
00:01:24,135 --> 00:01:26,324
Street, from strata, a paved road.

26
00:01:26,324 --> 00:01:27,483
Wall, from vallum.

27
00:01:27,483 --> 00:01:28,577
Wine, from vinum.

28
00:01:28,577 --> 00:01:30,122
And cheese, from caseus.

29
00:01:31,422 --> 00:01:35,374
Look at a map of England, and you can still see the Roman army.

30
00:01:35,374 --> 00:01:36,064
Manchester.

31
00:01:36,064 --> 00:01:36,691
Lancaster.

32
00:01:36,691 --> 00:01:37,381
Winchester.

33
00:01:37,381 --> 00:01:41,458
That ending, chester or caster, comes from the Latin word castra.

34
00:01:41,458 --> 00:01:43,026
It means a military camp.

35
00:01:44,326 --> 00:01:50,824
Then, around the year four hundred and ten, with the empire under attack at home, the last legions sailed away.

36
00:01:50,824 --> 00:01:52,815
Britain was left to defend itself.

37
00:01:52,815 --> 00:01:55,098
And that is where English truly begins.

38
00:01:56,398 --> 00:01:57,056
Chapter two.

39
00:01:57,056 --> 00:01:58,757
The invaders who named England.

40
00:02:00,057 --> 00:02:02,989
With Rome gone, new people crossed the North Sea.

41
00:02:02,989 --> 00:02:04,125
Jutes from Denmark.

42
00:02:04,125 --> 00:02:07,237
Saxons and Angles from what is now northern Germany.

43
00:02:07,237 --> 00:02:11,725
The old story says they first came as hired soldiers, and then they stayed.

44
00:02:13,025 --> 00:02:16,388
For more than a century, they fought the Britons for the land.

45
00:02:16,388 --> 00:02:22,410
Slowly, the newcomers pushed west, and the old Celtic languages retreated to Wales, Cornwall and the far north.

46
00:02:23,710 --> 00:02:26,104
The Angles gave the whole country its name.

47
00:02:26,104 --> 00:02:27,998
Englaland, the land of the Angles.

48
00:02:27,998 --> 00:02:32,007
Their language, Englisc, is the ancestor of every English word you know.

49
00:02:33,307 --> 00:02:35,976
The kingdoms they built are still on the map.

50
00:02:35,976 --> 00:02:37,340
Essex, the East Saxons.

51
00:02:37,340 --> 00:02:38,823
Sussex, the South Saxons.

52
00:02:38,823 --> 00:02:43,567
And Wessex, the West Saxons, the kingdom that would one day save English itself.

53
00:02:44,867 --> 00:02:46,380
They even named your week.

54
00:02:46,380 --> 00:02:47,893
Tuesday is the day of Tiw.

55
00:02:47,893 --> 00:02:49,522
Wednesday, the day of Woden.

56
00:02:49,522 --> 00:02:52,315
Thursday, the day of Thunor, the god of thunder.

57
00:02:52,315 --> 00:02:54,701
And Friday, the day of the goddess Frige.

58
00:02:56,001 --> 00:02:59,147
And the simplest words of daily life are theirs too.

59
00:02:59,147 --> 00:02:59,510
House.

60
00:02:59,510 --> 00:02:59,873
Bread.

61
00:02:59,873 --> 00:03:00,236
Water.

62
00:03:00,236 --> 00:03:00,660
Mother.

63
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:04,533
When you speak plainly in English, you are speaking Anglo-Saxon.

64
00:03:05,833 --> 00:03:09,807
But the Anglo-Saxons were about to learn what it feels like to be invaded.

65
00:03:09,807 --> 00:03:15,445
Because in the summer of seven hundred and ninety three, new ships appeared off the coast of Northumbria.

66
00:03:15,445 --> 00:03:18,077
And the men inside them were not coming to trade.

